// examples/1brc.nika
//
// The One Billion Row Challenge, in Nikaia 0.0.7.
//
// Input:  ~13 GB of lines, "<station name>;<temperature>\n", e.g. "Hamburg;12.0"
// Output: one line, "{Abha=-23.0/18.0/59.2, Abidjan=-16.2/26.0/67.3, ...}"
//         sorted by station name, min/mean/max to one decimal.
//
// This is a benchmark, not a demo: it stresses the three things the 0.0.7
// grammar protocol claims (scannerless parsing, zero-copy slices, `sync`
// parallelism). If it is slow, the argument in ADR-007 is wrong.
//
// Build:  nikaia build --user-parallelism=auto --release
// Run:    ./1brc measurements.txt
//
// This runs:
//
//     nikaia --input examples/1brc.nika --backend rust
//
// and `crates/nikaia/tests/one_brc.rs` compiles the result and checks what it
// prints, on this file rather than a copy of it.
//
// It does what it says: `fs::map` maps the file (ADR-014 D2), and under the
// `--user-parallelism=auto` the pieces are parsed on every core (D3) - 8 million lines
// on 4 cores took 0.14 s against 0.52 s sequentially, with identical output.
// Sequentially it is 659 instructions per row, against 840 for the same
// aggregation written naively in Rust and 688 for it hand-tuned (ADR-015 D3).
// `digit_value`, called for every digit below, is itself written in Nikaia and
// compiled into `std` by this compiler (D1).
//
// Where this file says something explicitly that a later Nikaia will infer -
// `as i64`, `&path`, `fn { … }` instead of `fn:` - the reason is in ADR-013 D7,
// and that last rewrite is why the `fn:` form is gone entirely (ADR-022).
// The compiler checks types (ADR-024) but converts nothing on its own: a
// printer that guessed would be worse than a source that says what it means.

use std::fs
use std::cli
use std::collections::HashMap

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. A grammar for the file
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// This is a grammar for the *input*, not for a line. You do not run it once per
// row - you write down what the file looks like and get a parser for it, which
// then drives itself over the bytes. Per-row invocation would pay the whole DSL
// protocol a billion times over; the parser pays it once.
//
// It is scannerless (Part II, 10.1): the rules consume the byte stream
// directly. There is no tokenizer to ask what a "token" is in `Hamburg;12.0`,
// and station names legitimately contain blanks, dots and non-ASCII text.
grammar Measurements {

    // Lexical rule (uppercase name): no implicit whitespace between parts.
    // That matters here - " Hamburg" and "Hamburg" are different stations, so
    // the parser must not helpfully skip the leading blank.
    //
    // The name is a slice into the input buffer, never a copy (Part II, 10.6).
    // At a billion rows, copying each name into a String is the difference
    // between seconds and minutes.
    //
    // Up to the semicolon - or to the end of the frame, whichever comes first.
    // `frame_end` is the boundary of the frame this rule is reached from
    // (MEASUREMENT below), written once there and referenced here. It has to
    // be said: a plain `until(";")` would run through a newline, and the
    // compiler rejects that under a frame rather than quietly fixing it
    // (ADR-009 D1). Either way it is one machine-word-at-a-time scan.
    rule NAME -> &str = s:until(";" | frame_end) -> { s }

    // Temperatures always carry exactly one decimal, so they are parsed as
    // tenths and the whole aggregation stays in integer arithmetic. Floating
    // point comes back only when printing.
    //
    // `digit{1,2}` states the width the format fixes (-99.9 ..= 99.9). That
    // is what lets the parser treat this as a fixed-width field rather than
    // scan for where the digits stop - and a three-digit temperature is a
    // parse error, as it should be (ADR-009 D5).
    rule TENTHS -> i32 =
        neg:"-"? whole:digit{1,2} "." frac:digit
        -> {
            let mut value = 0
            for d in whole.chars() { value = value * 10 + digit_value(d) }
            value = value * 10 + digit_value(frac)
            if neg.is_some() { -value } else { value }
        }

    // The commit point (`=>`) says: once the separator is there, this line *is*
    // a measurement. A malformed temperature after it is an error to report,
    // not a reason to backtrack and reinterpret the line. Without it a corrupt
    // row is silently skipped - the worst possible outcome for an aggregation.
    // `@frame` (Part II, 10.7) is what makes the parse parallel: it says a
    // measurement can be found from any offset by scanning to the next "\n",
    // so the file can be cut blindly and each piece repairs its own start. The
    // compiler does not take that on trust - it checks that no rule reachable
    // from here can consume a "\n" in the middle, which is exactly what would
    // make this wrong for, say, CSV with quoted fields. Uppercase, because a
    // frame is lexical: the implicit whitespace of a syntactic rule would eat
    // the very newline the file is cut at - and `Hamburg ; 12.0` is not a
    // measurement anyway.
    @frame(boundary: "\n")
    rule MEASUREMENT -> Reading =
        name:NAME ";" => temp:TENTHS frame_end
        -> { Reading { name, temp } }

    // The entry rule *is* the file.
    //
    // Writing `MEASUREMENT*` here would be wrong at this size: it collects, and
    // a billion Readings do not fit in memory. A fold threads an accumulator
    // through instead, so the parser consumes one measurement at a time and
    // nothing is ever collected - the parse runs in constant space.
    //
    // `par_fold` adds the second half of what parallel parsing needs: how two
    // partial results combine. With a frame boundary and a merge, the compiler
    // generates the split, the seam repair, one accumulator per core and the
    // final reduce - none of which appears in main() below.
    //
    // It has to be asked for. The compiler will not parallelise a plain `fold`
    // on its own, because a merge over floats is not associative and the core
    // count would silently change the answer. This one is associative: the
    // aggregation runs entirely in i64 tenths and only becomes f64 at print
    // time. Under --user-parallelism=0 the same grammar runs as a sequential fold.
    pub rule file -> Summary =
        par_fold(MEASUREMENT, Summary::new, fn(acc, m) { acc.record(m) },
                 Summary::merge)
}

// `@borrowed` is the assertion, not the mechanism: a `Reading` is built and
// consumed inside the fold, so its name stays a plain reference into the input
// buffer and costs nothing (Part I, 6.6). The marker makes that a compile-time
// promise instead of an expectation - if a later edit ever let a `Reading`
// escape the buffer's scope, the compiler would say so here rather than quietly
// putting a shared handle on a struct that is built a billion times
// (ADR-008, D6).
@borrowed
pub struct Reading {
    name: &str,
    temp: i32,
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Aggregation
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pub struct Stats {
    min: i32,
    max: i32,
    sum: i64,
    count: i64,
}

impl Stats {
    pub fn(first: i32) -> Stats sync {
        return Stats(min: first, max: first, sum: first as i64, count: 1)
    }

    // `sync` (Part II, 12.1): pure computation, cannot pause, cannot do I/O.
    // This is what lets the parser run inside `par_iter` below - the compiler
    // checks it, rather than us remembering it.
    fn add(&mut self, temp: i32) sync {
        if temp < self.min { self.min = temp }
        if temp > self.max { self.max = temp }
        self.sum += temp as i64
        self.count += 1
    }

    fn merge(&mut self, other: &Stats) sync {
        if other.min < self.min { self.min = other.min }
        if other.max > self.max { self.max = other.max }
        self.sum += other.sum
        self.count += other.count
    }

    fn mean(&self) -> f64 sync {
        return ((self.sum as f64) / (self.count as f64)) / 10.0
    }
}

// What one parser run produces: the stations it saw, and their statistics.
// The keys are slices into the input buffer too. `totals` dies before `data`
// does, so they are plain references here as well; were this summary returned
// past the mapping, the *map* would take one handle on the buffer and the keys
// would stay offsets - never one handle per key (ADR-008, D4).
pub struct Summary {
    stations: HashMap[&str, Stats],
}

impl Summary {
    pub fn() -> Summary sync {
        return Summary(stations: HashMap::new())
    }

    fn record(&mut self, m: Reading) sync {
        self.stations
            .entry(m.name)
            .and_modify fn { a.add(m.temp) }
            .or_insert_with fn { Stats(m.temp) }
    }

    fn merge(&mut self, other: Summary) sync {
        for (name, stats) in other.stations {
            self.stations
                .entry(name)
                .and_modify fn { a.merge(&stats) }
                .or_insert(stats)
        }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Main: the I/O half
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn main() throws {
    let path = cli::args().nth(1) ?? "measurements.txt"

    // I/O in direct style: no `async` on the signature, no `await` here.
    // `fs::map` is compiled into a non-blocking state machine like every other
    // `std::fs` call (Part III, 17.1). A memory map is what makes 13 GB
    // practical - the pages *are* the buffer, so nothing is copied and every
    // station name below points straight into the mapped file.
    //
    // `fs::map` needs a target with memory mapping, not a particular setting:
    // this would map the file just the same under `--user-parallelism=0` on Linux.
    // Only a `wasm32-*` target rules it out, where `fs::lines` is the
    // constant-memory alternative. Parallelism is asked for here so the
    // grammar's `par_fold` uses every core, not for the mapping - and at
    // `user_parallelism = 0` the same program still runs, on one core.
    let data = fs::map(&path) catch {
        eprintln(f"cannot open {path}: {error}")
        return
    }

    // The whole parse, on every core. There is no chunking code here because
    // the grammar already carries what chunking needs: `@frame` says where the
    // file may be cut, `par_fold` says how the pieces combine. One parser per
    // core, one accumulator each, nothing shared and nothing locked until the
    // merge - and a billion rows collapse to a few thousand stations, so that
    // merge costs nothing. A bad row in piece seven is reported at its line in
    // the file, not in piece seven (ADR-009 D3).
    //
    // Parsing is pure computation, so this satisfies the `sync` rule (12.1) the
    // parallel form requires. Had a `println` found its way into an action
    // block, the compiler would have said so here rather than quietly
    // serialising the workers.
    let totals = dsl Measurements from data

    let mut names = totals.stations.keys().collect()
    names.sort()

    let body = names
        .map fn {
            let s = &totals.stations[a]
            // 1BRC prints one decimal; a hole may say how it is written, after
            // the colon, as a format string does anywhere else.
            f"{a}={s.min as f64 / 10.0:.1}/{s.mean():.1}/{s.max as f64 / 10.0:.1}"
        }
        .join(", ")

    // `{{` and `}}` are literal braces: this one *is* an `f"…"`, so the escape
    // is the one a format string has always had. Only a string that
    // interpolates pays for it now (ADR-035 D1).
    println(f"{{{body}}}")
}
